--in points------------------->

“This was my last project at Infosys, where I worked for around 1.5 years. It was a completely Databricks-based project designed using a metadata-driven architecture.

We ingested data from multiple sources such as:
    Files (uploaded to Databricks volumes)
    Delta Sharing
    API calls

The goal was to process data daily and load it into corresponding tables in a scalable and automated way.”

High-Level Flow
“Whenever a file is uploaded to the Databricks volume, our orchestration layer detects it, identifies the file pattern, and fetches the corresponding metadata. Based on that, it triggers the appropriate processing job.”

Orchestration Layer
“We designed two main orchestrators:
1. File Orchestrator
    Continuously monitors the volume for new files
    Validates file name/pattern against metadata
    Fetches details like:
    Retailer info
    Granularity (daily/weekly/monthly)
    Job to be triggered
    Moves valid files to landing zone
    Inserts an entry in job metadata table with status as ‘PREPARING’
    Invalid files are moved to a rejected folder
2. Job Orchestrator
    Monitors job metadata table
    Picks jobs with ‘PREPARING’ status
    Checks for execution constraints (like retailer-level parallelism)
    Triggers the respective job with required parameters
    Updates job status to ‘STARTED’

Job Design (Dynamic Execution)
“We didn’t create separate jobs per file.
Instead, we designed jobs based on granularity (POS, daily, weekly, monthly).
Based on orchestrator inputs, the same job dynamically processes data for different retailers.”

Main Processing Flow (Medallion Architecture)
“Each job follows a layered architecture:

Bronze Layer
    Reads raw data from landing zone
    Stores it in bronze tables
Intermediate Layers
    Orphan handling
    Interim silver
Silver Layer
    Cleaned and transformed data
Post-processing
    Updates job status to completed
    Moves files to archive

If any step fails:
    A failure handler updates status to ‘FAILED’”
Job Tracking
    “We maintain a metadata table to track:

    Job start time
    End time
    Status (Preparing, Started, Completed, Failed)”
    
Code & Deployment
“All processing logic is written in Python and stored in GitHub.
    Unit testing is done using pytest
    Jobs are deployed using YAML configuration
    Databricks jobs are created based on these YAML files”

Delta Share & API Handling
Delta Share:
    Jobs are triggered based on status table entries
    Implemented CDC (Change Data Capture)
    Handled schema evolution
API:
    Scheduled jobs for data ingestion   
    Schema Evolution Handling

“We built dynamic transformations:
    Checked if source columns exist before applying transformations
    If missing → skipped transformation + sent notification
    Used mergeSchema = true while writing data”

Auto-Healing Framework
“We implemented an auto-healer job:
    Runs every 1 hour
    Picks failed jobs from metadata table
    Retries up to 3 times
    If still failing → sends alert to support team”

Key Highlights
✔ Fully metadata-driven pipeline
✔ Dynamic job execution
✔ Supports multiple data sources
✔ Built-in failure handling & auto-recovery
✔ Scalable and reusable architecture

